---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import EsyohWidget from '../../components/EsyohWidget.astro';
import AiSummary from '../../components/AiSummary.astro';
import Faq from '../../components/Faq.astro';
import { stateList, site } from '../../data/site';
import StateDirectory from '../../components/StateDirectory.astro';
import { allPrograms, stats } from '../../data/quality-index';
import bls from '../../data/bls.json';

const rdn = bls.occupations['dietitians-nutritionists'];

const counts = stateList.map(([slug, name]) => {
  const programs = allPrograms.filter(p => p.state === slug);
  return {
    slug,
    name,
    programs: programs.length,
    schools: new Set(programs.map(p => p.school)).size,
    acend: programs.filter(p => p.acendType.trim().length > 0).length,
    online: programs.filter(p => p.online).length
  };
});

const withPrograms = counts.filter(c => c.programs > 0).sort((a, b) => b.programs - a.programs);
const without = counts.filter(c => c.programs === 0);

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'Does it matter which state you study nutrition in?',
    answer:
      'More than students expect, for two reasons. Supervised practice placements are local, so a program with real relationships to hospitals and health departments in your region is worth more to you than a better-branded program two thousand miles away. And licensure is granted by states, with rules that differ, so studying where you intend to practise removes a set of problems you would otherwise have to solve later.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you use a nutrition degree from one state to work in another?',
    answer:
      'Generally yes. The RDN credential is national, granted by the Commission on Dietetic Registration, and accredited coursework from any state counts. What is not portable is licensure: each state board sets its own requirements and you apply separately. Check the board in the state you intend to work in before you assume the transition is automatic.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Which states have the most nutrition programs?',
    answer: `In our database, ${withPrograms.slice(0, 5).map(s => `${s.name} (${s.programs})`).join(', ')}. That distribution mostly tracks population and the number of public universities, not quality. A state with three programs may serve students better than one with twelve if the three have strong hospital relationships.`
  },
  {
    question: 'What if my state has no programs in your database?',
    answer: `Then either there genuinely are few or none, or our database has not captured them, and we would rather say that than pretend otherwise. Our coverage is ${stats.programs} programs at ${stats.schools} institutions across ${stats.states} states, and it is not exhaustive. If your state page is thin, check the neighbouring states listed on it and consider an accredited online program with local placement support.`
  },
  {
    question: 'Where do nutrition graduates get hired in each state?',
    answer:
      'Hospital systems, outpatient clinics, long-term care facilities, the state public health agency and its WIC program, county health departments, school districts, federally qualified health centers, food banks, and university extension services. Extension services are the most consistently overlooked employer in that list.'
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Nutrition Programs by State"
  description={`Nutrition and dietetics programs in all 50 states and the District of Columbia, with links to every official program page, state public health agency context, and local employer information.`}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Programs by state</p>
      <h1>Nutrition and dietetics programs by state</h1>
<EsyohWidget label="Programs accepting applicants now" intro="Accredited nutrition and dietetics programs currently taking applications." />
      <p class="lede">
        Where you study decides where you can complete supervised practice, and supervised practice decides your career.
        These pages list the programs in our database by state, with the public health agencies and employer categories
        that hire nutrition graduates locally.
      </p>
      <div class="metrics">
        <div><strong>{stats.programs}</strong><span>programs tracked</span></div>
        <div><strong>{stats.schools}</strong><span>institutions</span></div>
        <div><strong>{stats.states}</strong><span>states with a program in our database</span></div>
        <div><strong>51</strong><span>state pages, including DC</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <AiSummary>
        NutritionSchools.org publishes nutrition and dietetics program pages for all 50 US states and the District of
        Columbia. Our database holds {stats.programs} programs at {stats.schools} institutions across {stats.states}
        {' '}states, each linked to its official program page, with {stats.online} documenting an online option and
        {' '}{stats.accredited} institutions holding an ACEND accredited program. The states with the most programs in our
        database are {withPrograms.slice(0, 5).map(s => `${s.name} (${s.programs})`).join(', ')}. Each state page lists
        the programs we track, the state public health agency that administers WIC and community nutrition programs, the
        accredited supervised practice providers we can identify locally, and the employer categories that hire nutrition
        graduates in that state. The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported a May 2024 national median wage of
        ${rdn.median.toLocaleString()} for dietitians and nutritionists across about
        {' '}{rdn.employment.toLocaleString()} jobs. The RDN credential is national but licensure is granted state by
        state, so check your own board. Last reviewed {site.reviewedLabel}.
      </AiSummary>

      <div class="hot-take">
        <strong>Why geography matters more than ranking</strong>
        <p>
          Supervised practice placements are local relationships between a department and a hospital. A program in the
          region where you intend to work has those relationships. A more prestigious program two time zones away does
          not, and it cannot lend you the ones it has. This is the single most underweighted factor in program choice.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Every state</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Crawlable directory of all 50 states and the District of Columbia. Program counts below are from our own
        database and are not a claim of completeness.
      </p>
      <StateDirectory />
      <p class="lede" style="margin-top:32px">Counts for each jurisdiction:</p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Programs tracked</th><th>Institutions</th><th>At ACEND schools</th><th>Online option</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {counts.map(state => (
              <tr>
                <td><a href={`/states/${state.slug}/`}>{state.name}</a></td>
                <td>{state.programs || 'None in our database'}</td>
                <td>{state.schools || '0'}</td>
                <td>{state.acend || '0'}</td>
                <td>{state.online || '0'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="content wrap">
    <h2>How to use a state page</h2>
    <p>
      Start with the program list and open the official pages, because those are authoritative and our records are
      pointers to them. Then read the supervised practice section, which is the part that determines whether the program
      can actually get you credentialed. Then look at the employer categories, and go find live job postings from those
      employers to see what credentials they name.
    </p>
    <p>
      If your state has few or no programs in our database, that is worth knowing rather than hiding. Look at the
      neighbouring states listed on the page, and consider an accredited online program that arranges local placements.
      For many students in low-density states that is the only realistic route, and it is a legitimate one.
    </p>

    <h2>Licensure is a state matter</h2>
    <p>
      The RDN credential is national. Licensure is not. Some states license dietitians, some certify them, some regulate
      the title nutritionist, and some do not regulate it at all. The requirements to obtain a licence, and whether you
      may practise while an application is pending, differ.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is no substitute for reading your own state board rules, and there is no shortcut we can offer you that
      replaces it. Every state page names the public health agency; the licensing board is frequently a separate body and
      you should locate it specifically. See the
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/certifications/ln/">state licensure guide</a> for how to approach that.
    </p>

    <h2>States with the deepest coverage in our database</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Programs</th><th>Institutions</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {withPrograms.slice(0, 12).map(state => (
            <tr><td><a href={`/states/${state.slug}/`}>{state.name}</a></td><td>{state.programs}</td><td>{state.schools}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      That ordering reflects population and the density of public universities, not educational quality. Do not read a
      high count as an endorsement or a low count as a warning.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compare against the <a class="text-link" href="/rankings/">national rankings</a>,
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/rankings/online-nutrition-programs/">online nutrition programs</a>,
      {' '}<a class="text-link" href="/rankings/best-acend-accredited-nutrition-programs/">ACEND accredited programs</a>,
      and the <a class="text-link" href="/resources/nutrition-career-outcomes/">Career Outcomes Survey 2026</a>.
    </p>

    {without.length > 0 && (
      <>
        <h2>States where our coverage is thin</h2>
        <p>
          We have no programs recorded in {without.length} of the 51 jurisdictions:
          {' '}{without.map((s, i) => (
            <>
              {i > 0 ? ', ' : ''}
              <a href={`/states/${s.slug}/`}>{s.name}</a>
            </>
          ))}
          .
          Some of these genuinely have few or no institutions offering a nutrition or dietetics credential. In others our
          database has simply not captured what exists. We publish the gap rather than filling it with guesses, and each
          of those pages points you to the neighbouring states and to accredited online options.
        </p>
      </>
    )}

    <Faq items={faq} />

    <div class="notice">
      <strong>Coverage note:</strong> our database holds {stats.programs} programs at {stats.schools} institutions across
      {' '}{stats.states} states and is not a complete census of nutrition education in the United States. Program
      details, tuition, and accreditation status change. Every listing links to the institution's own page, which is the
      authoritative source, and ACEND is authoritative on accreditation.
    </div>
  </article>
<EsyohWidget label="Compare accredited programs" intro="Explore accredited nutrition and dietetics programs before you decide." />
</BaseLayout>
